// Deep-dive layer: Sirach. Format as in dd_a.js.
export default {
  72: [
    [1, `The fear of the Lord, and the root of wisdom`,
      `Sirach, written by Jesus ben Sira in Hebrew in Jerusalem about 180 BCE and translated into Greek by his grandson around 132 BCE (see the prologue), opens with a claim: “All wisdom comes from the Lord and is with him forever” (1:1). Wisdom was created before all things (1:4), and “the fear of the Lord is the beginning of wisdom” (1:14, compare Proverbs 1:7). Ben Sira then runs through four images: the fear of the Lord is “glory and exultation… gladness and a crown of rejoicing” (1:11), “the fulness of wisdom” (1:16), “the crown of wisdom” (1:18) and “the root of wisdom” (1:20). The book is a collection of teaching for young men in an educated Jewish setting under Hellenistic pressure. It is the longest wisdom book in the Bible’s wider collection and is read in Catholic and Orthodox lectionaries. The statement that fear of the Lord “is a delight” (1:12) is notable: this fear is not terror but reverent awe.`,
      [[`Proverbs 1:7`, `“The fear of the LORD is the beginning of knowledge.”`], [`Job 28:28`, `“The fear of the Lord, that is wisdom.”`]],
      [`Ben Sira lists four metaphors for the fear of the Lord. Which one reaches you most, and why?`, `The fear of the Lord brings gladness here. How do you reconcile fear and joy?`],
      [`choose`, `What does “fear of the Lord” mean in this chapter?`, `Reverent awe that shapes all of life.`, `Obedience to the law as an expression of loyalty.`]],
    [2, `Gold in the furnace`,
      `Sirach 2 gives instruction for those who seek to serve God: “My child, if you come forward to serve the Lord, prepare yourself for testing” (2:1). “Be sincere of heart and steadfast, and do not be impetuous in time of calamity” (2:2). “For gold is tested in the fire, and those found acceptable, in the furnace of humiliation” (2:5). The chapter repeats the verb “trust” and “hope”: “Trust in him, and he will help you; make your ways straight, and hope in him” (2:6). Three times it asks, “Who has ever trusted in the Lord and been disappointed?” (2:10). “Let us fall into the hands of the Lord, but not into the hands of mortals; for equal to his majesty is his mercy” (2:18). The advice is realistic about suffering and rooted in the story of Israel. Compare 1 Peter 1:6-7 and James 1:2-4.`,
      [[`1 Peter 1:6-7`, `Faith tested like gold in fire.`], [`2 Samuel 24:14`, `David chooses the hands of the LORD.`]],
      [`Ben Sira begins with warning, not comfort. How does it affect your reading of faith?`, `“Equal to his majesty is his mercy.” How does that sentence hold power and kindness together?`],
      [`choose`, `What is the purpose of testing in this chapter?`, `To prove and purify faithfulness.`, `To teach trust in God’s mercy.`]],
    [3, `Honour your father and mother`,
      `Sirach 3 starts with the fifth commandment expanded: “Whoever honours his father atones for sins, and whoever respects his mother is like one who lays up treasure” (3:3-4). “Those who honour their father will have joy in their own children, and when they pray they will be heard” (3:5). The text applies the command to old age: “O child, help your father in his old age, and do not grieve him as long as he lives; even if he is lacking in understanding, show forbearance; in all your strength do not despise him” (3:12-13). “Kindness to a father will not be forgotten” (3:14). The second half warns against intellectual pride: “Seek not what is too difficult for you, nor investigate what is beyond your power” (3:21). “What is committed to you, pay heed to; for what is hidden is not your concern” (3:22). The chapter ends: “Water extinguishes a blazing fire, so almsgiving atones for sin” (3:30).`,
      [[`Exodus 20:12`, `The fifth commandment.`], [`Tobit 12:8-9`, `Almsgiving delivers from death.`]],
      [`Ben Sira says to be patient with a father who is losing understanding. What does that look like when it is your reality?`, `“Seek not what is too difficult.” Where is a healthy limit on curiosity, and where does it become cowardice?`],
      [`rate`, `How well do you care for aging parents or mentors, in practice rather than intention?`, `Poorly`, `Well`]],
    [4, `Do not turn away from the poor`,
      `Sirach 4 begins with social ethics: “My child, deprive not the poor of his living, and do not keep needy eyes waiting. Do not grieve the hungry, nor anger one who is in need” (4:1-2). “Do not add to the troubles of the desperate, nor delay giving to the needy” (4:3). “Do not reject a suppliant in distress, nor turn your face away from the poor” (4:4). “Be a father to orphans, and be like a husband to their mother; you will then be like a son of the Most High, and he will love you more than does your mother” (4:10). Wisdom is personified as a teacher who tests her pupil (4:11-19). “Do not be ashamed to confess your sins, and do not try to stem the current of a river” (4:26). “Fight to the death for truth, and the Lord God will fight for you” (4:28). The chapter combines care for the vulnerable with courage in speech.`,
      [[`Deuteronomy 15:7-11`, `Open your hand to the poor.`], [`Isaiah 1:17`, `“Defend the fatherless, plead for the widow.”`]],
      [`“Do not delay giving to the needy.” What is the cost of delay in generosity?`, `Wisdom tests those who follow her before revealing her secrets. What tests have shaped you?`],
      [`choose`, `What is the main theme?`, `The ethical test of wisdom is care for the poor.`, `Wisdom is a hard teacher who demands trust.`]],
    [5, `Do not say, “His mercy is great”`,
      `Sirach 5 warns against self-sufficiency and presumption. “Do not rely on your wealth, or say, ‘I have enough’” (5:1). “Do not say, ‘Who will have power over me?’ for the Lord will surely punish you” (5:3). Then a specific caution against abusing mercy: “Do not be so confident of atonement that you add sin to sin. Do not say, ‘His mercy is great, he will forgive the multitude of my sins,’ for both mercy and wrath are with him, and his anger will rest on sinners” (5:5-6). “Do not delay to turn back to the Lord, nor postpone it from day to day” (5:7). The second half speaks of speech: “Do not winnow in every wind, or follow every path” (5:9). “Be steadfast in your understanding, and let your speech be consistent” (5:10). “Be quick to hear, but deliberate in answering” (5:11). The two-tongued person “is disgraced” (5:14). The passage resembles Romans 6:1.`,
      [[`Romans 6:1-2`, `“Are we to continue in sin that grace may abound?”`], [`James 1:19`, `“Quick to hear, slow to speak.”`]],
      [`Ben Sira warns against presuming on mercy. Where do you treat grace as a licence?`, `“Do not winnow in every wind.” How do you keep integrity of speech across different company?`],
      [`choose`, `What is the main danger described?`, `Presuming on God’s mercy to excuse delay.`, `Inconsistency of speech and character.`]],
    [6, `A faithful friend`,
      `Sirach 6 is the book’s great passage on friendship. “A sweet voice multiplies friends, and a gracious tongue multiplies courtesies” (6:5). “Let those who are at peace with you be many, but let your advisers be one in a thousand” (6:6). “Some friends are friends when it suits them, but they will not stay with you in times of trouble” (6:8). Then the central lines: “A faithful friend is a sturdy shelter; whoever finds one has found a treasure. There is nothing so precious as a faithful friend, and no scales can measure his excellence. A faithful friend is a life-saving remedy, and those who fear the Lord will find him” (6:14-16). Then wisdom’s yoke: “Put your feet into her fetters, and your neck into her collar. Bend your shoulder and carry her, and do not fret under her bonds” (6:24-25). “For at last you will find the rest she gives, and she will be changed into joy for you” (6:28). Compare Matthew 11:28-30.`,
      [[`Proverbs 18:24`, `“A friend who sticks closer than a brother.”`], [`Matthew 11:28-30`, `“Take my yoke upon you, and learn from me.”`]],
      [`Ben Sira says to test friends before trusting them. How do you tell a fair-weather friend from a faithful one?`, `Wisdom is a yoke that becomes rest. How does that image compare with Jesus’ invitation?`],
      [`rewrite`, `Write three sentences naming what a faithful friend has done for you, as if for the friend to read.`]],
    [7, `Do not sow injustice, and visit the sick`,
      `Sirach 7 collects short commands. “Do not do evil, and evil will never overtake you” (7:1). “Do not sow the furrows of injustice, and you will not reap a sevenfold crop” (7:3). “Do not seek from the Lord the highest office, nor the seat of honour from the king” (7:4). “Do not repeat yourself when you pray” (7:14). “Do not hate toil, or farm work, which was created by the Most High” (7:15). “Do not reject a sensible wife” (7:19). “Do not withhold kindness from the dead” (7:33). “Do not hesitate to visit the sick, because for such deeds you will be loved” (7:35). “In all you do, remember your end, and then you will never sin” (7:36). The advice on a wife, servants and parents shows the range of Ben Sira’s concerns: everyday relationships in a patriarchal household. The line on the dead recalls Tobit’s burial of the dead.`,
      [[`Matthew 25:36`, `“I was sick and you visited me.”`], [`Tobit 1:16-19`, `Tobit’s burial of the dead.`]],
      [`“Do not hate toil.” How does the chapter give dignity to ordinary work?`, `“Remember your end.” How could the thought of death shape ordinary choices?`],
      [`choose`, `What principle runs through the chapter?`, `Everyday acts of kindness and humility are the substance of wisdom.`, `The list is a miscellany with no single theme.`]],
    [8, `Be careful with the powerful, and keep old friends`,
      `Sirach 8-9 is a set of cautions about relationships. “Do not quarrel with the powerful, or you may fall into their hands” (8:1). “Do not quarrel with the rich, lest their resources outweigh yours” (8:2). “Do not scorn a person who is turning away from sin; remember that we all deserve punishment” (8:5). “Do not disregard the discourse of the sages” (8:8). “Do not reveal your heart to every person” (8:19). Chapter 9 gives a series of warnings about women, which reflect a patriarchal and fearful view of male desire: “Do not look intently at a virgin” (9:5); it is difficult for a modern reader. It ends with good advice on friends: “Do not forsake an old friend, for a new one does not compare with him. A new friend is like new wine; when it has aged you will drink it with pleasure” (9:10). The sections show a text that is wise on some matters and limited on others.`,
      [[`Proverbs 27:10`, `“Do not forsake your friend and your father’s friend.”`], [`Matthew 7:12`, `The golden rule in positive form.`]],
      [`Ben Sira’s caution about women reflects his society. How do you read an ancient text that is both wise and prejudiced?`, `“Remember that we all deserve punishment.” How does that sentence temper judgement of others?`],
      [`choose`, `How should we treat the sections on women?`, `As a historical witness to the limits of an ancient author.`, `As authoritative advice that must be balanced by other biblical texts.`]],
    [10, `The beginning of pride`,
      `Sirach 10 addresses rulers and the pride that ruins them. “A wise ruler educates his people, and the rule of an understanding person is well ordered” (10:1). “Sovereignty passes from nation to nation on account of injustice and insolence and wealth” (10:8). Then: “Why are the dust and ashes proud? Even in life the human body decays” (10:9). “The beginning of pride is for a man to forsake the Lord; his heart has been turned away from his Maker. For the beginning of sin is pride” (10:12-13). The Lord has overturned the thrones of rulers “and has seated the lowly in their place” (10:14). Honour is addressed: “Whoever is honoured in poverty, how much more in wealth!” (10:31). The sequence reads like a political commentary on a world of shifting empires and an assertion that humility is the safe course for a person.`,
      [[`Proverbs 16:18`, `Pride goes before destruction.`], [`Luke 1:52`, `“He has brought down the mighty from their thrones.”`]],
      [`“Why are the dust and ashes proud?” How does mortality shape the way you handle pride?`, `Sovereignty passes because of injustice. What empires of your time illustrate that?`],
      [`choose`, `What is the root of pride according to Ben Sira?`, `Turning away from God.`, `Forgetting one’s human limits.`]],
    [11, `Do not judge by appearance`,
      `Sirach 11 begins: “The wisdom of the humble lifts their heads high, and seats them among the great” (11:1). “Do not praise individuals for their good looks, or loathe anyone because of appearance alone. The bee is small among flying creatures, but what it produces is the best of sweet things” (11:2-3). “Do not boast about wearing fine clothes… for the works of the Lord are wonderful, and his works are concealed from humankind” (11:4). “Do not find fault before you investigate; examine first, and then criticise” (11:7). “Do not answer before you have heard” (11:8). The chapter then reflects on fortune: “Good things and bad, life and death, poverty and wealth, come from the Lord” (11:14). A warning about the rich man who says, “I have found rest, and now I shall feast on my goods,” not knowing “how much time will pass until he leaves them to others and dies” (11:19-20). It ends: “Call no one happy before his death” (11:28). Compare Luke 12:16-20.`,
      [[`Luke 12:16-21`, `The rich fool who plans to feast and dies that night.`], [`1 Samuel 16:7`, `“The LORD looks on the heart.”`]],
      [`“The bee is small, but what it produces is the best of sweet things.” What small thing in your life has outsized worth?`, `“Call no one happy before his death.” What does the saying suggest about how we evaluate a life?`],
      [`choose`, `What is the main point of 11:2-4?`, `Appearances do not reveal the value of a person.`, `Humility is the safest approach to status.`]],
    [12, `Do good, but know the enemy`,
      `Sirach 12-13 has two moods. In 12:1-7 Ben Sira urges, “If you do good, know to whom you do it, and you will be thanked for your good deeds. Do good to the devout, and you will be repaid” (12:1-2), then gives a harsh instruction: “Give to the good, but do not help the sinner” (12:4). The point is partly prudential: “Do not trust your enemy for ever, for like rust his wickedness recurs” (12:10). The text is uncomfortable next to Luke 6:35. Chapter 13 gives social observations: “Whoever touches pitch gets dirty” (13:1), “Every creature loves its like, and every person his neighbour” (13:15). “What fellowship is there between a wolf and a lamb? No more between the sinner and the devout. What peace is there between a hyena and a dog? And what peace between the rich and the poor?” (13:17-18). “The rich person does wrong and even adds insults; the poor suffers wrong and must add apologies” (13:3). The chapters reveal the realism and limits of Ben Sira’s class perspective.`,
      [[`Luke 6:32-36`, `“Love your enemies, and do good.”`], [`James 2:1-7`, `A warning against favouring the rich.`]],
      [`12:4 says not to help the sinner. How does it compare with Jesus’ teaching on enemies?`, `Ben Sira notices that the poor must apologise when wronged. What similar patterns do you see today?`],
      [`choose`, `How should we read the harder sayings?`, `As social wisdom of a particular culture, balanced by other texts.`, `As binding instruction within the wisdom tradition.`]],
    [14, `Enjoy life, and the happiness of wisdom`,
      `Sirach 14 begins with a blessing on the person whose “conscience does not condemn him” (14:2). It warns against stinginess: “A miser is not fit for riches; and what good is wealth to an envious person?” (14:3). “Whoever accumulates by depriving himself accumulates for others” (14:4). Then a perspective on mortality: “My child, treat yourself well, according to your means… Remember that death does not tarry” (14:11-12). “Give, and take, and indulge yourself, because in Hades one cannot look for luxury” (14:16). “All living beings become old like a garment, for the decree from of old is, ‘You must die!’” (14:17). The chapter then praises the person who meditates on wisdom: “Happy is the person who meditates on wisdom and reasons intelligently… who peers through her windows and listens at her doors” (14:20-23). The picture is of a lover waiting outside a house. The passage echoes Ecclesiastes 9:7-10.`,
      [[`Ecclesiastes 9:7-10`, `Enjoy life in the face of death.`], [`Psalm 1:1-2`, `Delight in the law and meditate day and night.`]],
      [`Ben Sira urges enjoyment before death. How do you balance enjoyment and generosity?`, `The image of listening at wisdom’s door is intimate. What would it mean to wait at wisdom’s door?`],
      [`choose`, `What is the tone of the chapter?`, `A realistic call to enjoy life within limits.`, `A call to wisdom as the deepest pleasure.`]],
    [15, `Choose life or death`,
      `Sirach 15 gives one of the Bible’s clearest statements on free will. “Do not say, ‘It was the Lord’s doing that I fell away,’ for he does not do what he hates” (15:11). “It was he who created humankind in the beginning, and he left them in the power of their own free choice” (15:14). “If you choose, you can keep the commandments, and to act faithfully is a matter of your own choice” (15:15). “He has placed before you fire and water; stretch out your hand for whichever you choose. Before each person are life and death, and whichever one chooses will be given” (15:16-17). “For great is the wisdom of the Lord; he is mighty in power and sees everything” (15:18). “He has not commanded anyone to be ungodly, and he has not given anyone permission to sin” (15:20). The passage was important in later debates between Augustine and Pelagius, and in Jewish discussions of free will. It echoes Deuteronomy 30:15-20.`,
      [[`Deuteronomy 30:15-20`, `“I have set before you life and death.”`], [`Joshua 24:15`, `“Choose this day whom you will serve.”`]],
      [`Ben Sira insists that blame cannot be shifted to God. How do you see that tendency in yourself?`, `“Fire and water” are before you. Which choice are you currently reaching for?`],
      [`choose`, `How should we think about freedom in this chapter?`, `As real moral choice before God.`, `As a limited freedom within God’s sovereignty.`]],
    [16, `Do not say, “I am hidden from the Lord”`,
      `Sirach 16 begins with a warning about children: “Do not desire a multitude of worthless children, nor rejoice in ungodly offspring” (16:1). “One can be better than a thousand, and to die childless is better than to have ungodly children” (16:3). The text recalls past judgements: the giants, Sodom, and the Canaanites (16:7-10). It then asks, “Do not say, ‘I am hidden from the Lord, and who is there to remember me from on high? Among so many people I shall not be known, for what am I in the boundless creation?’” (16:17). “Behold, heaven and the highest heaven, the abyss and the earth, tremble at his visitation” (16:18). The chapter ends with a reflection on creation’s order: “When the Lord created his works from the beginning, and, in making them, determined their boundaries, he arranged his works in an eternal order, and their dominion for all generations” (16:26-27). “They neither hunger nor grow weary, and they do not cease from their labours” (16:27).`,
      [[`Psalm 139:1-12`, `“Where shall I flee from your presence?”`], [`Genesis 19:24-25`, `Sodom’s destruction.`]],
      [`“Among so many people I shall not be known.” When do you feel unnoticed, and what does the text say?`, `The chapter pairs divine judgement with the order of creation. How do the two ideas fit?`],
      [`choose`, `What is the fault in “I am hidden from the Lord”?`, `A false belief that anonymity escapes accountability.`, `A despair that doubts God’s care.`]],
    [17, `The dignity and duty of humans`,
      `Sirach 17 rehearses the creation of humanity. “The Lord created human beings out of the earth, and makes them return to it again” (17:1). “He gave them a fixed number of days, but granted them authority over everything on the earth” (17:2). “He endowed them with strength like his own, and made them in his own image” (17:3). “He put the fear of them in all living beings, and gave them dominion over beasts and birds” (17:4). He gave them “counsel, and tongue and eyes, ears and a mind for thinking” (17:6), and “set his eye upon their hearts to show them the majesty of his works” (17:8). “He established with them an eternal covenant, and showed them his decrees” (17:12). The chapter then calls for repentance: “Return to the Lord and forsake your sins… Who will sing praises to the Most High in Hades in place of the living who give thanks?” (17:25-27). “How great is the mercy of the Lord, and his forgiveness for those who return to him!” (17:29).`,
      [[`Genesis 1:26-28`, `Image and dominion.`], [`Psalm 6:5`, `“In Sheol who will give you praise?”`]],
      [`Humans have dominion, a mind and a covenant. How does each shape responsibility?`, `The dead cannot praise. What does that suggest about the urgency of gratitude now?`],
      [`choose`, `What is the main claim of the first half?`, `That humans have dignity and authority from God.`, `That human life is limited and under God’s eye.`]],
    [18, `A person’s compassion and God’s compassion`,
      `Sirach 18 begins with God’s majesty: “He who lives forever created the whole universe” (18:1). “To whom has he given power to proclaim his works?” (18:4). “What are human beings, and of what use are they?… The number of a person’s days is great if it reaches a hundred years. Like a drop of water from the sea and a grain of sand, so are these few years in the day of eternity” (18:8-10). “Therefore the Lord is patient with them and pours out his mercy upon them” (18:11). Then the line: “A person’s mercy is for his neighbour, but the Lord’s mercy is for every living thing. He rebukes and trains and teaches them, and turns them back, as a shepherd his flock” (18:13). The practical teachings include: “Before you speak, learn” (18:19), “Before you fall ill, take care of your health” (18:19-20), “Remember the time of hunger in the time of plenty” (18:25), and “Do not let your gift become a reproach” (18:15).`,
      [[`Psalm 103:13-18`, `As a father shows compassion.`], [`Proverbs 19:17`, `Lending to the poor is lending to the LORD.`]],
      [`“A drop of water from the sea.” How does the image of a human life shape what you worry about?`, `“Remember the time of hunger in the time of plenty.” What would that look like practically?`],
      [`choose`, `What is the key contrast in 18:13?`, `Limited human mercy and God’s universal mercy.`, `The shepherd’s correction and his kindness.`]],
    [19, `Words, gossip, and cleverness`,
      `Sirach 19 is about speech and character. “Wine and women lead intelligent men astray, and the man who consorts with prostitutes is reckless” (19:2). “A person who trusts others too quickly is lightminded” (19:4). “Have you heard a word? Let it die with you. Be brave! It will not make you burst” (19:10). “With a fool, a word makes him suffer like a woman in labour giving birth” (19:11). “Question a friend, perhaps he did not do it; but if he did, so that he may do it no more. Question a neighbour, perhaps he did not say it; but if he said it, so that he may not say it again” (19:13-14). Sirach distinguishes wisdom from cleverness: “There is a cleverness that is detestable, and there is a fool who merely lacks wisdom” (19:23). “Better is the God-fearing person who lacks intelligence than the highly prudent person who transgresses the law” (19:24).`,
      [[`Proverbs 11:13`, `A gossip reveals secrets.`], [`James 3:1-12`, `The power of the tongue.`]],
      [`“Let it die with you.” When have you kept a confidence, and when have you not?`, `Ben Sira prefers a faithful person with limited intelligence over a clever transgressor. Is that a fair ranking?`],
      [`choose`, `What does the chapter offer about friendship?`, `Confront gently, in case you have misjudged.`, `Do not spread rumours.`]],
    [20, `When to speak, and when to be silent`,
      `Sirach 20 offers a catalogue of sayings on speech. “There is a reproof that is untimely, and there is a person who keeps silent and is wise” (20:1). “How much better it is to reprove than to stay angry! And the one who confesses his fault will be kept from loss” (20:2-3). “One person keeps silent and is thought to be wise, while another is detested for being too talkative” (20:5). “There is a time to be silent and a time to speak” (20:6). “A thief is better than an habitual liar, but both will inherit disgrace” (20:25). “A proverb from a fool’s mouth will be rejected, for he does not tell it at the proper time” (20:20). “A gift of a fool will profit you nothing, for his eyes are many instead of one” (20:14). The chapter shows an interest in timing and appropriateness, a central concern of wisdom literature, and compares to Ecclesiastes 3:7.`,
      [[`Ecclesiastes 3:7`, `“A time to keep silence, and a time to speak.”`], [`Proverbs 25:11`, `“A word fitly spoken.”`]],
      [`“There is a time to be silent and a time to speak.” How do you know which one it is?`, `The chapter treats the timing of a proverb as part of its truth. What advice have you received at the wrong moment?`],
      [`choose`, `What is the key skill described?`, `Discernment of the right time to speak.`, `Avoidance of foolish speech.`]],
    [21, `The fool and the wise`,
      `Sirach 21 begins with sin: “Have you sinned, my child? Do so no more, but pray about your past sins” (21:1). “Flee from sin as from a snake; for if you approach sin, it will bite you” (21:2). “All lawlessness is like a two-edged sword; there is no healing for its wound” (21:3). The second half is a series of contrasts between fools and wise people. “The mind of a fool is like a broken jar; it will hold no knowledge” (21:14). “When a skilled person hears a wise saying, he will praise it and add to it; when a profligate hears it, he dislikes it and tosses it behind his back” (21:15). “A fool’s conversation is like a burden on a journey, but delight will be found in the speech of the intelligent” (21:16). “The heart of fools is in their mouth, but the mouth of the wise is in their heart” (21:26). The chapter turns on whether a person receives correction.`,
      [[`Proverbs 12:1`, `“Whoever loves discipline loves knowledge.”`], [`James 4:17`, `“Whoever knows the right thing to do and fails to do it, for him it is sin.”`]],
      [`“The mouth of the wise is in their heart.” What does it mean to speak from the heart rather than say whatever comes to mind?`, `How do you respond to wise sayings: add to them, or toss them behind your back?`],
      [`rate`, `How receptive are you to correction?`, `Defensive`, `Open`]],
    [22, `Guard over my mouth`,
      `Sirach 22 contrasts the lazy, the foolish and the faithful. “The lazy are like a filthy stone, and everyone hisses at their disgrace” (22:1). “The mourning for the dead lasts seven days, but for the fool and the ungodly it lasts all their lives” (22:12). “Do not talk much with a fool, and do not visit an unintelligent person” (22:13). “Sand, salt, and a lump of iron are easier to bear than a stupid person” (22:15). On friendship: “Whoever pricks an eye brings tears, and whoever pricks the heart makes it show feeling” (22:19). “If you have drawn your sword against a friend, do not despair, for a return is possible. If you have opened your mouth against your friend, do not worry, for reconciliation is possible” (22:21-22). At the chapter’s close, a prayer: “O that a guard were set over my mouth, and an effective seal upon my lips, that I may not fall because of them, and that my tongue may not destroy me!” (22:27). The prayer continues in chapter 23.`,
      [[`Psalm 141:3`, `“Set a guard over my mouth, O LORD.”`], [`Proverbs 17:9`, `“Whoever covers an offence seeks love.”`]],
      [`Ben Sira says reconciliation after harsh words is possible except when a friend’s secret is betrayed. Why is that the exception?`, `Seven days are given to mourn the dead. How does grief shape the way you give time to others?`],
      [`rewrite`, `Write a prayer of two lines for a guard over your own mouth in a specific situation.`]],
    [23, `Prayer, oaths and adultery`,
      `Sirach 23 opens with a prayer: “O Lord, Father and Ruler of my life, do not abandon me to their counsel, and do not let me fall because of them” (23:1). He asks God to set discipline over his thoughts: “Who will set whips over my thoughts, and the discipline of wisdom over my mind, so that they may not spare me in my errors?” (23:2). Then a warning on oaths: “Do not accustom your mouth to oaths, and do not habitually utter the name of the Holy One” (23:9). On sexual sin, he describes the adulterer who says, “Who sees me? Darkness surrounds me, and the walls hide me” (23:18), not realising that “the eyes of the Lord are ten thousand times brighter than the sun” (23:19). The chapter ends with the effect on a woman who commits adultery, and the claim that “those who are left behind will recognise that nothing is better than the fear of the Lord, and nothing sweeter than to heed the commandments of the Lord” (23:27).`,
      [[`Matthew 6:9`, `“Our Father in heaven, hallowed be your name.”`], [`Matthew 5:33-37`, `“Let your ‘Yes’ be ‘Yes.’”`]],
      [`Ben Sira addresses God as “Father” in a Jewish prayer. How does that compare with later usage?`, `“Who sees me?” What do you do when you are certain no one sees?`],
      [`choose`, `What is the underlying concern?`, `Discipline of thought and speech before God’s all-seeing eye.`, `Social stability protected by moral rules on sexuality.`]],
    [25, `Three things, and a bad wife`,
      `Sirach 25 starts with a numerical saying: “My soul takes pleasure in three things, and they are beautiful in the sight of the Lord and of mortals: agreement among brothers, friendship among neighbours, and a wife and a husband who live in harmony” (25:1). It then lists people he hates, including “a poor man who is proud” and “a rich man who is a liar” (25:2). The second half contains some of the book’s most severe lines about women: “Any wound, but not a wound of the heart! Any wickedness, but not the wickedness of a woman!” (25:13). “From a woman sin had its beginning, and because of her we all die” (25:24). The text uses a reading of Genesis 3 that places blame on Eve. Paul assigns responsibility in different ways in Romans 5:12-14 and 1 Corinthians 15:21-22. These lines are among the passages that have led readers to criticise Ben Sira’s view of women, though he also praises a good wife in chapter 26.`,
      [[`Genesis 3:1-19`, `The fall narrative.`], [`Romans 5:12-14`, `Adam as the origin of sin and death.`]],
      [`Ben Sira lays the origin of sin on Eve; Paul on Adam. What does the difference show about interpretive choices?`, `How do you read a text that includes both a lovely saying about harmony and a harsh saying about women?`],
      [`choose`, `How should a modern reader weigh Sirach 25:24?`, `As a limited reading of Genesis that scripture itself corrects.`, `As part of the book’s patriarchal setting, to be named plainly.`]],
    [26, `A good wife, and two things that grieve`,
      `Sirach 26 begins with a blessing: “Happy is the husband of a good wife; the number of his days will be doubled” (26:1). “A loyal wife brings joy to her husband, and he will complete his years in peace” (26:2). “A good wife is a great blessing; she will be granted as a blessing to the one who fears the Lord” (26:3). The passage praises a gracious, silent, skilled wife: “A gracious wife delights her husband, and her skill puts flesh on his bones” (26:13). “Like the sun rising in the heights of the Lord, so is the beauty of a good wife in her well-ordered home” (26:16). The chapter then warns about unfaithful or shameless women (26:6-12, 22-27), using the same terms of control and suspicion. The final section lists things that distress him: “At two things my heart is grieved, and because of a third anger comes over me: a warrior in want through poverty, and intelligent men who are treated with contempt, and a person who turns back from righteousness to sin” (26:28).`,
      [[`Proverbs 31:10-31`, `The woman of valour.`], [`Genesis 2:18-24`, `Woman as a partner and helper.`]],
      [`The chapter praises a good wife and fears a bad one in the same breath. What does that say about his anxieties?`, `The final list ends with a person who returns to sin. Why is that grief placed beside poverty and contempt?`],
      [`choose`, `How do you read the chapter’s treatment of women?`, `As praise within the limits of a patriarchal horizon.`, `As a contribution to a long tradition of reflection on marriage.`]],
    [27, `Money, speech, and fruit`,
      `Sirach 27 begins with a sober saying on money: “Many have sinned for gain, and those who seek to get rich avert their eyes” (27:1). “A peg will stick fast between the joints of stones, and sin will be wedged in between buying and selling” (27:2). “The fruit of a tree discloses the cultivation; so the expression of a thought discloses the cultivation of a person’s mind. Do not praise anyone before he speaks, for this is the test of a person” (27:6-7). “If you pursue justice, you will attain it and wear it like a splendid robe” (27:8). “Birds roost with their kind, so truth returns to those who practise it” (27:9). On friendship and secrets: “Whoever betrays secrets destroys confidence, and he will never find a close friend” (27:16). On malice: “Anger and wrath are abominations, yet a sinner holds on to them. The vengeful will face the Lord’s vengeance, for he keeps a strict account of their sins” (27:30-28:1). The idea that we receive what we dispense anticipates Matthew 6:14-15.`,
      [[`Matthew 7:16-20`, `A tree is known by its fruit.`], [`Matthew 6:14-15`, `Forgive, and be forgiven.`]],
      [`“Do not praise anyone before he speaks.” What does your speech reveal about your cultivation?`, `The passage ties vengefulness to being vengefully treated by God. How does that compare with the Lord’s Prayer?`],
      [`choose`, `What does the tree image teach?`, `Speech shows character.`, `Habits and upbringing shape the person.`]],
    [28, `Forgive, and guard the tongue`,
      `Sirach 28 gives one of the best-known teachings on forgiveness in pre-Christian literature. “The vengeful will face the Lord’s vengeance… Forgive your neighbour the wrong he has done, and then your sins will be pardoned when you pray” (28:1-2). “Does anyone harbour anger against another, and expect healing from the Lord?” (28:3). “If he has no mercy toward a person like himself, can he seek pardon for his own sins?” (28:4). “Remember the end of your life, and set enmity aside; remember destruction and death, and be true to the commandments” (28:6). “Remember the commandments, and do not be angry with your neighbour; remember the covenant of the Most High, and overlook faults” (28:7). The second half deals with the tongue: “A blow from a whip raises a welt, but a blow from the tongue crushes the bones” (28:17). “Fence in your property with thorns, lock up your silver and gold; make balances and scales for your words, and make a door and a bolt for your mouth” (28:24-25).`,
      [[`Matthew 6:12-15`, `Forgiveness tied to being forgiven.`], [`Matthew 18:21-35`, `The unforgiving servant.`]],
      [`“Forgive your neighbour… then your sins will be pardoned when you pray.” How does that relate to Jesus’ teaching?`, `“Make balances and scales for your words.” What would it be to weigh your words before speaking?`],
      [`rate`, `How easily do you release grievances, in comparison with holding them?`, `Hold`, `Release`]],
    [29, `Lending, almsgiving and guests`,
      `Sirach 29 discusses lending and almsgiving. “The merciful lend to their neighbours; by holding out a helping hand they keep the commandments” (29:1). “Lend to your neighbour in his time of need; and in turn, repay your neighbour promptly” (29:2). Yet many treat a loan as a windfall and cause trouble for those who helped them (29:4-7). “Because of the commandment, help the poor; and because of their need, do not send them away empty-handed” (29:9). “Lose your silver for the sake of a brother or a friend, and do not let it rust under a stone and be lost” (29:10). “Store up almsgiving in your treasury, and it will rescue you from every disaster” (29:12). The last section is on hospitality: “The essentials for life are water, bread, and clothing, and also a house to ensure privacy” (29:21). “Better is the life of the poor under the shelter of their own roof than sumptuous food in another person’s house” (29:22). The chapter shows an economy based on lending among neighbours and the pressure that creates.`,
      [[`Deuteronomy 15:7-11`, `Do not harden your heart toward a poor brother.`], [`Luke 6:34-35`, `“Lend, expecting nothing in return.”`]],
      [`Ben Sira states that people often treat loans as windfalls. How does that complicate generosity?`, `“Store up almsgiving.” How is giving a form of treasure?`],
      [`choose`, `How does the chapter combine generosity and realism?`, `It urges lending while warning about those who exploit it.`, `It treats almsgiving as spiritual insurance.`]],
    [30, `Disciplining children, and a merry heart`,
      `Sirach 30 starts with child-rearing: “Whoever loves his son will whip him often, so that he may rejoice at the way he turns out” (30:1). It reflects a harsh view of discipline typical of its time: “Do not laugh with him, lest you have sorrow with him” (30:10). The principle behind it is concern for character: “A horse that is untamed turns out to be stubborn, and a son who is left to himself turns out to be wilful” (30:8). Then health: “Better off is the poor person who is healthy and fit than the rich person who is severely afflicted in body” (30:14). “Health and fitness are better than any gold, and a robust body than countless riches” (30:15). “Do not give yourself over to sorrow, and do not distress yourself deliberately” (30:21). “Gladness of heart is the life of man, and the rejoicing of a man is length of days” (30:22). “Delight your soul and comfort your heart… for sorrow has destroyed many” (30:23). “Jealousy and anger shorten life, and anxiety brings on premature old age” (30:24). The links between health and emotion appear modern.`,
      [[`Proverbs 13:24`, `“Whoever spares the rod hates his son.”`], [`Proverbs 17:22`, `“A joyful heart is good medicine.”`]],
      [`Ben Sira endorses corporal punishment. How should a modern reader respond to such counsel?`, `He says a cheerful heart lengthens life. What do you find in your own experience?`],
      [`choose`, `How should we approach the discipline advice?`, `As a cultural norm of its time that we may revise in light of other texts.`, `As permanent guidance about firmness in raising children.`]],
    [31, `Wealth, food and wine`,
      `Sirach 31 begins with the costs of wealth: “Wakefulness over wealth wastes away one’s flesh, and anxiety about it drives away sleep” (31:1). “The rich person toils to amass a fortune, and when he rests he fills himself with his delicacies” (31:3). “Gold has ruined many, and their destruction was at hand” (31:6). “Blessed is the rich person who is found blameless, and who does not go after gold” (31:8). The second half is etiquette for the table: “Are you seated at a great table? Do not be greedy at it, and do not say, ‘There is a lot of food here!’” (31:12). “Judge your neighbour’s feelings by your own” (31:15). “Eat what is set before you like a well-mannered person” (31:16). On wine: “Wine is very life to human beings if taken in moderation. What is life to one who is without wine? It has been created to make people happy” (31:27). “Wine drunk to excess leads to bitterness of spirit, to quarrels and stumbling” (31:29). The advice resembles Greek symposium manners adapted to Jewish ethics.`,
      [[`Ecclesiastes 5:10-12`, `“The sleep of a labourer is sweet.”`], [`Psalm 104:15`, `“Wine to gladden the heart.”`]],
      [`“Judge your neighbour’s feelings by your own.” How does that serve as a general rule?`, `Ben Sira praises wine in moderation. What does his balance say about enjoyment and restraint?`],
      [`choose`, `What is the common thread?`, `Moderation in wealth and appetite.`, `A guide to behaviour at table in a Hellenistic setting.`]],
    [32, `At the banquet, and think before you act`,
      `Sirach 32 offers advice for the host and guests at a banquet. “If they make you master of the feast, do not exalt yourself; be among them as one of them. Take good care of them and then be seated” (32:1). “When you have fulfilled your duties, take your place, so that you may be merry on their account and receive a wreath for your excellent leadership” (32:2). The older guests speak first, “for it is fitting for you” (32:3), but without interrupting the music (32:3-6). “Speak, young man, if there is need of you, but no more than twice, and only if asked” (32:7). Then wise advice: “Do nothing without deliberation; and when you have acted, have no regrets” (32:19). “Do not go on a path full of hazards, and do not stumble over stony ground” (32:20). “Whoever keeps the law preserves himself” (32:23). The advice ends on trust: “The one who trusts the Lord will not be harmed” (32:24).`,
      [[`Luke 14:7-11`, `Jesus on seats at a banquet.`], [`Proverbs 14:15`, `“The prudent gives thought to his steps.”`]],
      [`The host should serve first and then sit. How does that picture leadership?`, `“Do nothing without deliberation, and have no regrets.” How do these two work together?`],
      [`choose`, `What does the chapter assume about social roles?`, `Humble leadership at table models wise conduct.`, `Age and status set the order of speaking.`]],
    [33, `Opposites, clay, and slaves`,
      `Sirach 33 opens: “No evil will befall the one who fears the Lord, but in trial he will be rescued again and again” (33:1). Then a reflection on order in creation: “Look at all the works of the Most High; they come in pairs, one the opposite of the other” (33:15). It speaks of God’s choices in terms of the potter and clay: “Like clay in the hand of the potter, to be moulded as he pleases, so all are in the hand of their Maker, to be given whatever he decides” (33:13). “Good is the opposite of evil, and life the opposite of death; so the sinner is the opposite of the godly” (33:14). Elsewhere (15:14) free will is stressed, and the combined picture is a tension, not a contradiction. Verses 25-33 give counsel on slaves: “Fodder, a stick, and burdens for a donkey; bread, discipline, and work for a slave” (33:25), and also, “Treat a slave who has proven his worth as a brother” (33:31). The text reflects the moral limits of its social world.`,
      [[`Jeremiah 18:1-10`, `The potter and the clay.`], [`Philemon 15-16`, `A slave “no longer as a slave but more than a slave.”`]],
      [`Ben Sira uses the potter image while elsewhere insisting on free will. How do you hold both?`, `The chapter shows a master’s attitude to slaves. How should a reader treat a text that endorses a structure we reject?`],
      [`choose`, `How should we read “the works come in pairs”?`, `As a statement of divine order that includes contrasts.`, `As a rhetorical device for emphasising moral choice.`]],
    [34, `Dreams, and sacrifice from the poor`,
      `Sirach 34 is sceptical about dreams: “Vain and false hopes are for the senseless, and dreams give wings to fools” (34:1). “As one who catches at a shadow and pursues the wind, so is anyone who believes in dreams” (34:2). “Divinations, omens, and dreams are all unreal” (34:5). The chapter praises experience: “An educated person knows many things, and one with much experience will speak with understanding” (34:9). Then a powerful ethical text: “Like one who kills a son before his father’s eyes is the person who offers a sacrifice from the property of the poor” (34:24). “The bread of the needy is the life of the poor; whoever deprives them of it is a murderer” (34:25). “To take away a neighbour’s living is to commit murder; to deprive an employee of wages is to shed blood” (34:26-27). “If one builds and another tears down, what do they gain but toil?” (34:23). The chapter shows a concern for the just economy as a condition of worship.`,
      [[`Amos 5:21-24`, `“Let justice roll down like waters.”`], [`Deuteronomy 24:14-15`, `Do not oppress a hired servant.`]],
      [`Ben Sira says that unjust gain makes worship worthless. How do you see that principle in your own context?`, `The chapter doubts dreams while the Bible contains many. How do you hold the two together?`],
      [`choose`, `What is the main point of 34:24-27?`, `Worship cannot be separated from just dealing.`, `Wage theft is a form of bloodshed.`]],
    [35, `A sacrifice that pleases, and the prayer of the humble`,
      `Sirach 35 defines worship: “The one who keeps the law makes many offerings; one who heeds the commandments sacrifices a peace offering” (35:1). “Whoever returns a kindness offers fine flour, and whoever gives alms sacrifices a thank offering” (35:2). “To keep from wickedness is pleasing to the Lord, and to forsake unrighteousness is an atonement” (35:3). “Do not appear before the Lord empty-handed” (35:4), and “do not offer a bribe, for the Lord will not accept it” (35:14). The Lord is “a God of justice, and with him there is no partiality” (35:15). “He does not show partiality to the poor but he listens to the prayer of one who is wronged. He does not ignore the supplication of the orphan, or the widow when she pours out her complaint” (35:16-17). “The prayer of the humble pierces the clouds” (35:21). “It will not rest until it reaches its goal; and it will not desist until the Most High responds” (35:21). “The Lord will not delay” (35:22).`,
      [[`Micah 6:6-8`, `What does the LORD require?`], [`Luke 18:1-8`, `The persistent widow and God’s justice.`]],
      [`Ben Sira equates obedience with sacrifice. How does that relate to the temple and its offerings?`, `The prayer of the humble “will not rest until it reaches its goal.” What prayer of yours is still going?`],
      [`choose`, `What is the key claim about God?`, `God listens to the oppressed and delivers justice.`, `Proper worship includes ethical life.`]],
    [36, `A prayer for Israel`,
      `Sirach 36 is a prayer for the deliverance of Israel, apparently written during political oppression. “Have mercy upon us, O Lord, the God of all, and look on us, and show us the light of your mercy” (36:1). “Send fear upon all the nations” (36:2). “Lift up your hand against foreign nations and let them see your might” (36:3). “As you have been sanctified among us in their sight, so be glorified among them in our sight” (36:4). “Give new signs, and work other wonders” (36:6). “Gather all the tribes of Jacob, and give them their inheritance, as at the beginning” (36:11). “Have mercy on the people called by your name, on Israel, whom you have named your firstborn” (36:17). “Fill Zion with your majesty, and your temple with your glory” (36:19). “Bear witness to those whom you created in the beginning, and fulfil the prophecies spoken in your name” (36:20). The chapter ends with advice on choosing a wife (36:21-26). The prayer mirrors the Eighteen Benedictions of later Jewish liturgy.`,
      [[`Psalm 79:1-13`, `A prayer after the nations desecrate Jerusalem.`], [`Isaiah 11:11-12`, `The gathering of the dispersed.`]],
      [`The prayer asks God to gather the tribes and fill the temple. What do you ask for on behalf of your own people?`, `The prayer calls for judgement on nations. How do you hold such a prayer alongside love of enemies?`],
      [`choose`, `What is the prayer’s main hope?`, `The restoration of Israel and recognition of God by the nations.`, `Relief from foreign domination.`]],
    [37, `Counsellors, friends, and the wise`,
      `Sirach 37 begins with false friends: “Every friend says, ‘I too am a friend’; but some friends are friends only in name” (37:1). “Is it not a grief to the death when a companion and friend turns to enmity?” (37:2). On counsel: “Let the counsel of your own heart stand, for no one is more faithful to you than it is” (37:13). “For a person’s mind sometimes keeps him better informed than seven sentinels sitting high on a watchtower” (37:14). “Above all, pray to the Most High that he may direct your way in truth” (37:15). Every counsellor offers advice, but some advise in their own interest (37:7). He lists the kinds of unreliable advisers: the ones who work for personal gain or have no interest. “A wise person instructs his own people, and the fruits of his understanding are trustworthy” (37:23). “Test yourself while you live; see what is bad for you and do not give it to yourself” (37:27). The chapter ends with temperance at the table.`,
      [[`Proverbs 11:14`, `Many advisers bring safety.`], [`Psalm 1:1`, `“Blessed is the man who walks not in the counsel of the wicked.”`]],
      [`“Some counsel in their own interest.” How do you test whether advice is shaped by the adviser’s interest?`, `Ben Sira says to pray for direction. How do you combine prayer with seeking counsel?`],
      [`choose`, `What does the chapter value most?`, `A discerning heart supported by prayer and a few trustworthy advisers.`, `Independence in decision-making.`]],
    [39, `The scribe who studies the law`,
      `Sirach 38:24 to 39:11 is a famous portrait of the scribe. After describing the labour of ploughmen, smiths and potters, Ben Sira says the scribe, who has leisure, devotes himself to study: “He seeks out the wisdom of all the ancients, and is concerned with prophecies; he preserves the discourse of the famous and penetrates the subtleties of parables” (39:1-3). “He serves among the great and appears before rulers; he travels through foreign lands and learns what is good and evil in the human lot” (39:4). “If the great Lord is willing, he will be filled with the spirit of understanding; he will pour forth words of wisdom of his own and give thanks to the Lord in prayer” (39:6). “Many will praise his understanding; it will never be blotted out” (39:9). The second half of the chapter is a hymn on God’s works: “All the works of the Lord are very good, and whatever he commands will be done in due time” (39:16). “The works of all flesh are before him; nothing can be hid from his eyes” (39:19). “Good things were created for the good from the beginning, and so evil things for sinners” (39:25). The chapter is a rare picture of an ancient school-teacher’s self-understanding.`,
      [[`Ezra 7:10`, `Ezra the scribe set his heart to study the law.`], [`Genesis 1:31`, `“God saw everything that he had made, and behold, it was very good.”`]],
      [`Ben Sira values the scribe above the craftsperson because of leisure. How do you judge the dignity of different kinds of work?`, `The scribe studies law, wisdom and prophecy. What would be your equivalent reading list?`],
      [`choose`, `What is the portrait’s main point?`, `Wisdom requires time and devotion to tradition.`, `A defence of scribal learning as a way of serving God.`]],
    [40, `Life’s troubles, and what lasts`,
      `Sirach 40 opens with a reflection on human misery: “Hard work was created for everyone, and a heavy yoke is laid on the children of Adam, from the day they come forth from their mother’s womb until the day they return to the mother of all the living” (40:1). The same fate befalls all: “From the one who sits on a splendid throne to the one who is humbled in dust and ashes” (40:3). “Anger, jealousy, trouble, and unrest, and fear of death, and fury and strife” (40:4). Then a list of things that bring joy: “Wine and music gladden the heart, but the love of wisdom is better than both” (40:20). “The flute and the harp make pleasant melody, but a pleasant voice is better than both” (40:21). “The eye desires grace and beauty, but more than both the green shoots of the crop” (40:22). “Friend and companion never meet amiss, but better than both is a wife with her husband” (40:23). “Kindness is like a garden of blessings, and almsgiving endures forever” (40:17). “My child, do not lead the life of a beggar; it is better to die than to beg” (40:28).`,
      [[`Job 5:7`, `“Man is born to trouble.”`], [`Ecclesiastes 2:22-23`, `Toil and sorrow of the heart.`]],
      [`Ben Sira lists everyone’s troubles, then lists better things. What is the effect of the “better than” pattern?`, `“Almsgiving endures forever.” How does generosity outlast other pleasures?`],
      [`choose`, `What is the main movement?`, `From universal affliction to the things that console.`, `A catalogue of observations about life.`]],
    [41, `Death, and shame`,
      `Sirach 41 starts with a meditation on death: “O death, how bitter is the thought of you to the person at peace amid possessions, who has nothing to worry about and is prosperous in everything” (41:1). “O death, how welcome is your sentence to one who is needy and failing in strength, worn down with age and anxious about everything” (41:2). “Do not fear the sentence of death; remember those who went before you and those who will come after” (41:3). “This is the decree from the Lord for all flesh; why then should you reject the will of the Most High?” (41:4). Ben Sira turns to the reputation that outlasts: “Have regard for your name, since it will outlive you longer than a thousand great stores of gold” (41:12). “The days of a good life are numbered, but a good name lasts forever” (41:13). Then a long list of things to be ashamed of and not ashamed of (41:14-42:8). “Be ashamed of immorality before your father or mother… be ashamed of a lie before a prince” (41:17-18).`,
      [[`Ecclesiastes 7:1`, `“A good name is better than precious ointment.”`], [`Proverbs 22:1`, `“A good name is to be chosen over great riches.”`]],
      [`Death is bitter to the prosperous and welcome to the needy. How does one’s situation shape the experience of death?`, `“A good name lasts forever.” What do you want your name to mean?`],
      [`choose`, `What is the best reading of “Do not fear the sentence of death”?`, `A reassurance that death is a universal human fate.`, `A call to focus on reputation and legacy.`]],
    [42, `Do not be ashamed, and the works of the Lord`,
      `Sirach 42 continues the list of what not to be ashamed of: the law of the Most High, accuracy in scales, a gift to friends, discipline of children (42:1-8). Then comes a section on daughters, which reflects the anxious protectiveness of a father in that culture (42:9-14). The text turns at 42:15 to a hymn on God’s works: “I will now call to mind the works of the Lord, and will declare what I have seen. By the words of the Lord his works are made” (42:15). “The sun looks down on everything with its light, and the work of the Lord is full of his glory” (42:16). “He searches out the abyss and the human heart; he understands their innermost secrets” (42:18). “All of them live and remain forever for every need, and all are obedient” (42:23). “All things come in pairs, one opposite the other, and he has made nothing incomplete” (42:24). “One supplements the virtues of the other. Who could ever tire of beholding his glory?” (42:25). The following chapter develops this hymn at length.`,
      [[`Psalm 104:24`, `“O LORD, how manifold are your works.”`], [`Job 38:4-7`, `Creation’s order.`]],
      [`Ben Sira begins a creation hymn after chapters of social advice. How do ethics and wonder fit together?`, `“He has made nothing incomplete.” How does that sentence challenge a sense of waste?`],
      [`choose`, `What is the main purpose of the hymn?`, `To praise the Creator through his ordered works.`, `To introduce the praise of famous men that follows.`]],
    [43, `The glory of creation`,
      `Sirach 43 is a hymn in praise of nature. “The pride of the heavenly heights is the clear firmament, the appearance of heaven in a spectacle of glory” (43:1). “The sun, when it appears, proclaims at its rising what a marvellous instrument it is, the work of the Most High” (43:2). “At noon it parches the land; and who can withstand its burning heat?” (43:3). “The moon marks the changing seasons, governs the times, and is a sign for the world” (43:6). “Look at the rainbow, and praise its Maker; it is exceedingly beautiful in its brightness. It encircles the heaven with a glorious ring; the hands of the Most High have stretched it out” (43:11-12). He describes snow, hail, wind and frost as God’s instruments (43:13-22) and the sea: “Those who sail the sea tell of its dangers” (43:24). At the end: “We could say more but could never say enough; the sum of our words is: ‘He is the all’” (43:27). “Where can we find the strength to praise him? For he is greater than all his works” (43:28). “Glorify the Lord as much as you can, for he surpasses even that” (43:30).`,
      [[`Psalm 148:1-14`, `All creation praises the LORD.`], [`Job 37:1-24`, `The wonders of the weather.`]],
      [`Ben Sira ends by saying words cannot suffice. When have you run out of words for wonder?`, `“He is the all.” How do you read the phrase without collapsing God into nature?`],
      [`rewrite`, `Describe one natural phenomenon you have seen, in four lines, as an act of praise.`]],
    [44, `Let us now praise famous men`,
      `Sirach 44-50 is a “Praise of the Fathers,” a survey of Israel’s heroes. It opens: “Let us now sing the praises of famous men, our ancestors in their generations. The Lord apportioned to them great glory, his majesty from the beginning” (44:1-2). It lists rulers, counsellors, prophets, musicians and poets (44:3-6). “All of these were honoured in their generations, and were a glory in their time” (44:7). “Some of them have left behind a name, so that others declare their praise. But of others there is no memory; they have perished as though they had never existed; they have become as though they had never been born, they and their children after them” (44:8-9). “But these also were godly men, whose righteous deeds have not been forgotten” (44:10). Enoch, Noah, Abraham, Isaac and Jacob follow (44:16-23). The collection is organised by covenants: with Noah, Abraham, and later with Aaron and David. The list is notable for what it includes, such as Enoch and Simon the priest, and for what it omits, such as Ezra, Daniel and Mordecai.`,
      [[`Hebrews 11:1-40`, `A New Testament roll call of the faithful.`], [`Psalm 78:1-8`, `Telling the next generation the glorious deeds of the LORD.`]],
      [`Some people are remembered and others are forgotten. How do you honour those who have no memorial?`, `The list is structured around covenants. What pattern do you see in whom he includes?`],
      [`choose`, `What is the purpose of the survey?`, `To root the present in the heritage of Israel’s heroes.`, `To argue for a certain model of priesthood and leadership.`]],
    [45, `Moses, Aaron and Phinehas`,
      `Sirach 45 describes Moses first: “He brought forth from his descendants a man of mercy, who found favour in the sight of all flesh and was beloved by God and people, Moses, whose memory is blessed” (45:1). “He made him equal in glory to the holy ones, and made him great in the fears of his enemies” (45:2). Aaron receives more space: Ben Sira describes his vestments in detail, his anointing, and the “everlasting covenant” of the priesthood (45:6-22). The ephod, breastplate and golden bells are described as “a memorial for his people” (45:9-11). “He exalted Aaron, a holy man like Moses, his brother of the tribe of Levi” (45:6). Phinehas, the son of Eleazar, is praised for his zeal: “He was the third in glory for being zealous in the fear of the Lord, and stood fast, when the people turned away, in the loyalty of his good and ready soul, and made atonement for Israel” (45:23). The weight given to priestly figures reflects Ben Sira’s own priestly sympathies.`,
      [[`Exodus 28:1-43`, `The priestly garments.`], [`Numbers 25:6-13`, `The covenant of peace with Phinehas.`]],
      [`Aaron receives more attention than Moses. What does that tell you about the author’s values?`, `The priestly vestments are described like a work of art. What function do beauty and ritual serve for a community?`],
      [`choose`, `What does the emphasis on Aaron suggest?`, `Ben Sira’s support for the priestly establishment of his day.`, `A theology of worship as the heart of Israel’s life.`]],
    [46, `Joshua, the judges and Samuel`,
      `Sirach 46-47 continues the praise. Joshua “was mighty in war, and was the successor of Moses in prophesying. He became, in accordance with his name, a great saviour of God’s elect” (46:1). Caleb receives praise for his strength in old age (46:7-10). The judges are named: “May their bones send forth new life from where they lie, and may the name of those who have been honoured live again in their sons” (46:12). Samuel “was beloved by his Lord, a prophet of the Lord, who established the kingdom and anointed rulers over his people” (46:13). He is praised for his integrity, “Before the Lord and his anointed he testified: ‘I have not taken anyone’s property, not even a pair of sandals,’ and no one accused him” (46:19). The following chapter turns to Nathan, David and Solomon (47:1-22). David’s early victories and his psalms are praised: “In all that he did he gave thanks to the Holy One Most High, with words of glory; he sang praise with all his heart, and he loved his Maker” (47:8). Solomon’s wisdom is praised, and his failures are named: “You laid your loins beside women, and through your body you were brought into subjection” (47:19).`,
      [[`1 Samuel 12:1-5`, `Samuel’s declaration of integrity.`], [`1 Kings 11:1-13`, `Solomon’s turning away.`]],
      [`Ben Sira names Solomon’s failure alongside his wisdom. What does it do for the credibility of his praise?`, `“Not even a pair of sandals.” How do you measure integrity in leadership?`],
      [`choose`, `What stands out about Ben Sira’s method?`, `A brief portrait of each person, mixing praise and honest critique.`, `A focus on leaders who established institutions.`]],
    [48, `Elijah, Elisha, Hezekiah and Isaiah`,
      `Sirach 48 begins: “Then the prophet Elijah arose like a fire, and his word burned like a torch” (48:1). He brought famine on the people, called down fire, and was “taken up by a whirlwind of fire, in a chariot with horses of fire” (48:9). “At the time appointed, it is written, you are destined to calm the wrath of God before it breaks out in fury, to turn the hearts of parents to their children, and to restore the tribes of Jacob” (48:10), recalling Malachi 4:5-6. “Happy are those who saw you, and those who have been adorned in love, for we also shall surely live” (48:11). Elisha “was filled with the spirit of Elijah” (48:12). Hezekiah “did what was pleasing to the Lord” and “strengthened his city by bringing water into it” (48:17), a reference to the Siloam tunnel. Isaiah “saw by the spirit of might what would occur at the end of time, and comforted those who mourned in Zion” (48:24).`,
      [[`Malachi 4:5-6`, `Elijah to return before the day of the LORD.`], [`2 Kings 20:20`, `Hezekiah’s tunnel.`]],
      [`Elijah is expected to “restore the tribes of Jacob.” How does that expectation shape the way New Testament writers speak of John the Baptist?`, `Ben Sira praises Isaiah as one who comforted the mourning. What is the role of a comforter in a community?`],
      [`choose`, `What does Ben Sira stress about Elijah?`, `His fiery power and his role in the end times.`, `His ascent as a hope for the faithful.`]],
    [50, `Simon the high priest`,
      `Sirach 50 concludes the praise of the fathers with a contemporary figure, Simon son of Onias the high priest (probably Simon II, c. 219-196 BCE). He repaired the temple, fortified it and dug a large cistern (50:1-4). Then comes a lyrical description of him emerging from the sanctuary on the Day of Atonement: “How glorious he was when the people gathered round him as he came out of the house of the curtain! Like the morning star among the clouds, like the full moon at the festal season; like the sun shining on the temple of the Most High” (50:5-7). “When he put on his glorious robe and clothed himself in perfect splendour, when he went up to the holy altar, he made the court of the sanctuary glorious” (50:11). The people then fall down in worship. The chapter ends with a blessing and a note on two nations Ben Sira detests and a third that is not a nation (50:25-26): the Edomites, Philistines and the foolish people living in Shechem. It closes: “Instruction in understanding and knowledge I have written in this book, Jesus son of Eleazar son of Sirach” (50:27).`,
      [[`Leviticus 16:1-34`, `The Day of Atonement.`], [`Hebrews 9:11-14`, `Christ as high priest emerging from the sanctuary.`]],
      [`Ben Sira describes the high priest like a heavenly body. What does the splendour tell you about his view of worship?`, `The chapter names the author. What does it mean for a wisdom teacher to sign his work?`],
      [`choose`, `What is the significance of the Simon portrait?`, `It shows a contemporary leader as a continuation of the long line of heroes.`, `It reveals the importance of the temple liturgy to Ben Sira.`]],
    [51, `A prayer, and a poem about seeking wisdom`,
      `Sirach 51 has two parts. First, a thanksgiving of the author: “I give thanks to you, O Lord and King, and praise you, O God my Saviour” (51:1). He recalls being rescued from slander, danger and the pit of Hades (51:2-12). Then an autobiographical poem on the search for wisdom: “While I was still young, before I went on my travels, I sought wisdom openly in my prayer. In front of the temple I asked for her, and I will search for her until the end” (51:13-14). “From blossom to ripening grape, my heart delighted in her; my foot walked on the straight path; from my youth I followed her steps” (51:15). “I bent my ear a little and received her, and I found for myself much instruction” (51:16). “Draw near to me, you who are uneducated, and lodge in my school” (51:23). “Put your neck under the yoke, and let your souls receive instruction; it is near to find her” (51:26). “See with your eyes that I have laboured little and found for myself much rest” (51:27). The poem’s acrostic form in the Hebrew text found at Qumran shows a lyrical craft not visible in translation.`,
      [[`Matthew 11:28-30`, `“Come to me… and I will give you rest.”`], [`Proverbs 8:17`, `“Those who seek me diligently find me.”`]],
      [`Ben Sira invites students into his school. How does the invitation compare with Jesus’ words about yoke and rest?`, `He seeks wisdom from youth and into old age. What does a lifelong pursuit of wisdom look like for you?`],
      [`choose`, `What does the closing poem accomplish?`, `It shows the personal devotion behind the book’s instruction.`, `It is an invitation to future students.`]]
  ]
};
